# 0003. The repo is the live config, one config for macOS and Arch

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-05

`~/.config/nvim` is a symlink to this repo, and the same files serve the Mac and Arch, with
OS differences handled inline.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the same editor is used on a Mac and an Arch desktop and should behave the same
  on both, with one place to change it.
- **Link**: `make link` symlinks `$(HOME)/.config/nvim` to the repo. An existing config (file,
  dir or other link) is moved to `~/.config/nvim.bak-<YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS>`, never deleted;
  re-running is a no-op when already linked. `make unlink` removes the link and restores the
  newest backup.
- **Install**: `make install` = `tools` (`scripts/install-tools.sh`: brew on macOS; pacman,
  apt or dnf on Linux: neovim, ripgrep, fd, fzf, tree-sitter CLI, node, go, formatters) +
  `link` + `sync` + `lsp` + `notebook`.
- **OS differences inline**: `vim.fn.has("mac")`, never separate branches or files. Example:
  `detect_os()` in `lua/plugins/ui.lua` returns `mac`, `arch` (from `/etc/os-release`) or
  `linux` and picks the start-screen logo, wordmark (`archminimal`, `apple silicon` /
  `intel mac`, or the distro name) and statusline icon.
- **Flow**: changes go through branches and PRs; `main` is what every machine runs. After a
  merge: `git pull` on each machine, `make sync` if `lazy-lock.json` changed.
- **Consequences**: one source of truth; `git pull && make sync` brings a machine up to date.
  The checked-out branch is the editor in use right now: a feature branch left checked out
  is live and never reaches the other machine, so switch back to `main` after pushing.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: GNU Make, Homebrew / pacman / apt / dnf.
